\description{
Fit the detection link of a latent-scale mixed model (how a cell's latent value, such as the log-odds of a site's occupancy, sets the chance that a trial succeeds) by maximum likelihood over cells with a latent mean and SD and a count.

Answers \strong{how does a count's chance of success follow the latent value?} mzLib's
\code{LogitNormalMixedModel.FitLink} (mzLib #1469) finds the \code{DetectionLink} - logit g = intercept +
slope x latent + depth_slope x depth - that makes your cells' counts most likely, each cell's
latent value taken as a normal with the mean and SD you give, by maximum likelihood. By default
it fits on \strong{detection}: whether any trial succeeded, which is what makes a covered zero. Pass
the result to \code{\link{stats_fit_latent_mixed}} as \code{link}.
}
\details{
A slope below 1 says the chance of a success rises more slowly than the latent: on PTM data, for
example, whether a modified form is seen depends on how much of it there is, not only on its
share. \strong{The cells, and their latent means and SDs, are your choice}: mzLibR does not build them.
mzLib's own example is a covered cell's feature mean and SD over its measured cells.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_link_fit}: \code{cells_file}, the cells table's absolute path; \code{cell_count}, rows
  read (cells); \code{depth_given}, whether the cells file has a \code{depth} column; \code{with_depth},
  \code{with_overdispersion} and \code{detection_only}, as passed: what you gave, else mzLib's declared
  default; \code{link}, the fitted \code{mzlibr_detection_link}, ready for \code{\link{stats_fit_latent_mixed}}'s
  \code{link}, or \code{NULL} when mzLib's search did not converge - \code{intercept} (natural log-odds, the
  per-trial log-odds at latent 0 and depth 0), \code{slope} (natural log-odds per latent unit),
  \code{depth_slope} (natural log-odds per depth unit; exactly 0 when \code{with_depth} is \code{FALSE}) and
  \code{overdispersion} (fraction, the intra-cell correlation, 0 to below 1; exactly 0 when
  \code{with_overdispersion} is \code{FALSE}); and \code{caveats}, what the fit is and is not.

}
\section{What it is not}{


A point estimate only: mzLib returns no standard errors. The cells and their latent normals are
modelling choices, and the link fitted from them is only as good as they are. With
\code{with_depth = TRUE} and no depth column, every depth is 0 and the data say nothing about
\code{depth_slope}.

}
